 {:seon.task/name "done-reads-its-reason-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md, S14; still true in src/seon/server.clj:171-179). What exists: the :done op waits with a pred that tests the run status and c/defect, and after the wait tests the same two facts again to choose its answer (status in #{:running :ended} and defect, at 174 and 178). Correct: the wait returns the reason it ended on (the pred returns the verdict or the defect it matched), and :done answers that, so one read of the facts decides. Replaces the second read. Proof to write: :done answers the defect the wait matched even when main holds a later commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-tools-are-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: the MCP tools are thin wrappers around Seon's REPL-based eval, so an outside session (a Claude) works as a Seon agent does and sees what it sees. Inside, a Seon agent has one tool, eval (prose and code at its REPL), and every capability is a function it evaluates: (my.agent/prove), (my.agent/messages), (my.agent/problem sig), (my.agent/help ..), @ctx; each answer is one shape by the one printer (handle, value or error, :out, and a line of what changed: :main). Rule: every capability is a function an eval calls, none is only a tool. Outside: seon_eval {agent, prose, code} (an agent's id or root) is the tool, through seon.repl/eval-string, origin :outside, its text the printed response a Seon agent's model reads; seon_attach {task} stays (a session needs a process, and attach records its client as holder); the ops agents, context, message, prove and done become functions evaluated on root or the agent ((my.root/agents), (my.agent/context), (my.agent/send-message ..), (my.agent/prove), (my.task/done!)) and their seon.server ops and op-answer methods are deleted, bin/ scripts and checks calling seon_eval. Each answer adds what its caller cannot see otherwise: for a Seon agent, the problems its eval caused (a test child that died in its prove, a refused write, a failed effect: root's records against that eval); for an outside client (bin/mcp stamps its client id), root's problem facts changed on main since that client's last answer (the main t it last saw, held by the server as a cache keyed by client, regenerable: read through Datahike's history, never my.root/problems' ~600 MB recomputation), as problems-model's grouped rows. A worktree's root is one more bin/mcp entry (its port argument), no new mechanism. Also from the 2026-10-30 proposal: a dead agent's end, exit and stderr tail answered by the agents function (root records them, seon.root's end-agent and record-death); one named test proved through the prove function (names take ns/test as bin/test does). Owned by the optimization session with the context session (the owner, 2026-10-30)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
